# OpenDiving - instance configuration.
#
# Save this next to docker-compose.yml as `.env`, edit the six values in the first block,
# and run `docker compose up -d`. Everything below that block already has a working
# default; the settings shown commented out are the ones worth knowing exist.
#
# Two of these refuse to start the stack rather than run on a value copied from a
# template: SECRET_KEY, and ADMIN_PASSWORD if you enable the admin panel. Anything
# commented out is commented out on purpose - uncommenting a block is a decision.
#
# The full reference is https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving/tree/main/docs/configuration.md, and `src/.env.example` in
# `opendiving-api` lists every API setting that exists.

# ============================================================================
# The six values to edit
# ============================================================================

# 1. The address this instance is reached at - hostname only, no scheme, no path. Point
#    its DNS A/AAAA record at this machine *before* the first `docker compose up`: the
#    bundled Caddy gets a Let's Encrypt certificate on startup, and it can only do that
#    for a name that already resolves here.
#
#    It is also where every emailed sign-in link points (the API's FRONTEND_URL and the
#    web app's SITE_URL are both derived from it in docker-compose.yml), so a wrong value
#    is not cosmetic. No domain? See "LAN / no domain" at the bottom.
#
#    And it is the passkey domain: browsers scope a passkey to the hostname it was created
#    under, so moving this name later leaves every passkey already registered unusable.
#    Nobody is locked out - the emailed link is the way back in, and everyone re-adds one -
#    but it is silent. See docs/configuration.md#sign-in.
DOMAIN=dives.example.com

# 2. Signs every access, refresh and onboarding token the API issues, so anyone holding
#    this value can mint one for any account. Generate your own:
#
#      openssl rand -hex 32
#
#    The placeholder below is published in this repository and the API REFUSES TO START
#    on it. Rotating it later signs every existing session out.
SECRET_KEY=change-me-openssl-rand-hex-32

# 3. The database password. Read once, when the `db` volume is first created - changing
#    it later edits this file and nothing else (docs/troubleshooting.md says
#    how to actually change it). Generate one:
#
#      openssl rand -base64 24
#
#    A password containing @ / : or # also has to be percent-encoded in the derived
#    CRUD_ADMIN_DB_URL in docker-compose.yml, so if you plan to enable the admin panel,
#    generating one without them saves a step.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# 4-6. Where outgoing mail goes. Sign-in is passwordless - an emailed link, or the
#    six-digit code beside it, is how anybody including you first gets in - so an instance
#    with ENVIRONMENT set to anything but local and no SMTP_HOST refuses to start rather
#    than discovering this at the first sign-in attempt. Invitations are emailed too, so
#    on a new install (which is invite-only by default) the relay is what everybody after
#    you depends on twice: once for their invitation, once for the link that signs them in.
#
#    Point this at a relay you already trust: your mail provider, your host's relay, or
#    your own existing mail server. Don't stand up an MTA for it unless you already know
#    why - sending mail is easy, getting it *accepted* (PTR, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, IP
#    reputation, and a port 25 your host probably blocks) is the hard part.
#
#    EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS must be an address on a domain your relay is allowed to send for.
#    There is no default: a wrong one fails at delivery time, hours later, in somebody's
#    spam folder. The username and password are optional and independent - a relay that
#    authenticates by IP needs neither, and the login is only attempted when a username is
#    set.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=noreply@example.com
# starttls (587, the default) | tls (465) | none (a relay on localhost only). Certificates
# are verified in both TLS modes.
# SMTP_TLS_MODE=starttls
#
# Resend users: SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com, SMTP_USERNAME=resend, and SMTP_PASSWORD is the
# API key.

# ============================================================================
# How this instance is served
# ============================================================================

# Runs the bundled Caddy, which terminates TLS and is the only container publishing a
# port. Comment this line out if you already run Traefik, nginx or Nginx Proxy Manager on
# this box - two proxies cannot both hold 80/443 - then point yours at `web:3000` and read
# docs/reverse-proxy.md, which covers the two settings that matter when you
# do (this one and TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS below).
COMPOSE_PROFILES=proxy

# Which proxies' X-Forwarded-For header the API believes, and every per-IP rate limit
# depends on it. This is the compose network's subnet, pinned in docker-compose.yml, which
# is where the bundled Caddy sits.
#
# Running your own proxy? Add its address or network here - on the compose network if you
# attach it to this stack, or its host address if it reaches the app another way. Get it
# wrong and every caller looks like the proxy: all the per-IP buckets merge into one, and
# a single bot exhausting the magic-link limit locks sign-in for everybody.
#
# It also decides whose `X-Forwarded-Proto` is believed (docker-compose.yml passes it on
# as FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS), which is what keeps the admin panel from redirecting to HTTPS
# it is already on. One list, both jobs - and the reason to write each entry as either a
# bare address (10.1.2.3) or a network whose host bits are zero (10.0.0.0/8). The
# in-between shape, 10.1.2.3/8, means the block to this app and is refused outright by the
# server that reads the second setting: the container then exits with
# `Error: 10.1.2.3/8 has host bits set`.
TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=172.29.0.0/16

# Makes the API a production instance: the interactive docs at /docs disappear, and
# SMTP_HOST becomes mandatory. `staging` differs only in putting those docs behind a
# superuser - it requires a relay just as production does. Set it to `local` only for a
# throwaway instance you are poking at: `local` is the one value that falls back to
# logging the sign-in link instead of emailing it, which is a sensible way to try the app
# and a terrible way to run it, since that link is a working credential for whoever reads
# the logs.
ENVIRONMENT=production

# The version to run. `latest` follows every release; pin it to a version (and change it
# deliberately, after reading the release notes) once this instance holds dives you care
# about. Both images - api and web - always move together on the same number.
# OPENDIVING_VERSION=latest

# DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL, applied to the API and the worker alike.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ============================================================================
# Who may create an account
# ============================================================================

# This instance is invite-only unless you say otherwise, which is what the commented-out
# line below changes. On the default, "invite", only an address somebody already invited
# can create an account: a stranger who opens the home page gets a "request an invite"
# form in place of the sign-in form, and their address joins a queue you work through.
# On "open" anybody who proves they own an address gets an account - which is what this
# app did before this setting existed.
#
# Leave it closed if this instance is reachable from the internet and you only mean to
# share it with a handful of dive buddies. That is the case the default is chosen for.
#
# **The first account on an empty instance is admitted whichever value this has**, and it
# is created with the operator's rights - the "first account to sign in is yours" sentence
# in the install docs, and the reason closed-by-default is not a first-run trap. Sign in
# once and everybody else arrives by an invitation: every member has an Invitations card
# in Settings, and your account also has an Admin entry in the account menu, which lists
# the addresses that used the request form.
#
# An invitation is an entry against the email address, not a code to forward: the invitee
# signs in with the address that was invited, so there is nothing for them to lose. And
# nothing here changes how anybody signs in - the link, the code, Google and passkeys are
# exactly as they were.
# REGISTRATION_MODE=open

# How many invitations one member may send, and over how long. A rate rather than a
# lifetime allotment: at the defaults, somebody who invites five friends today can invite
# five more tomorrow. Counted from the invitations themselves - revoked ones included -
# so the bound is on how many emails they caused to be sent. You are exempt.
# INVITATIONS_PER_USER=5
# INVITATIONS_WINDOW_DAYS=1

# A separate backstop on how often one member may *attempt* an invitation, over
# MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. Deliberately looser than the quota above and not
# the same thing: inviting an address that already has an account is refused without
# creating anything, so the quota never charges for it, and this is what stops that
# refusal being a way to probe which addresses are registered. Nobody is exempt.
# INVITATION_ATTEMPT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=20

# Fixed-window rate limits on the request-an-invite form, keyed by the submitted address
# and by the caller's IP. That endpoint takes an anonymous stranger's word for it and
# writes a row, which is what these are for; the per-IP one is only as good as
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS above. Consulted only while REGISTRATION_MODE is "invite".
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# INVITE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=10

# ============================================================================
# Optional
# ============================================================================

# Where the web app's contact form delivers to. Unset, that endpoint answers 503 and the
# form is simply off - which is the right default for an instance whose users are you and
# your dive buddies, and better than mailing their support requests to strangers.
# CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL=you@example.com
# Shown on the contact page as a fallback if a submission fails. Display only; set it only
# if it matches the address above.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Google Sign-In, offered alongside the magic link. Both halves of one OAuth 2.0 client
# from the Google Cloud Console, "Web application" type. Set both or neither: an id with no
# secret makes the API refuse to start, naming them, because signing in with Google redeems
# an authorization code at Google's token endpoint and that takes the secret.
#
# The client ID is not a secret - it travels in a URL the visitor can read, and the web
# container gets a copy. The client secret is one, and stays on the API: it is deliberately
# absent from the `web` service's `environment:` block in docker-compose.yml, and nothing in
# the browser half of the flow has any use for it.
#
# You must also register {FRONTEND_URL}/auth/google/callback as an Authorized redirect URI
# on that client - Google refuses the exchange otherwise, and sign-in answers 401. That is
# the API's own setting, not the web app's SITE_URL beside it: the two default to the same
# https://${DOMAIN} and can be set apart, and it is FRONTEND_URL the API derives from. An
# existing client from an older release will have no redirect URI at all, because the
# previous flow never sent one. See "Setting up Google sign-in" in
# docs/configuration.md.
#
# Setting the id costs a signed-out visitor nothing. No page loads anything of Google's and
# nothing reaches Google until somebody presses the button; pressing it navigates the browser
# out to Google's own site, which is what signing in with Google means, and the code it comes
# back with is redeemed by this API rather than in the browser. Unset, the button is hidden
# and the bundled privacy page has no Google section at all.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret

# The basemap behind every map the web app draws - the dive-site and trip forms, a dive
# site's or trip's own page, and a dive that has a position. Set nothing and it draws the
# MapLibre vector styles the image ships (OpenFreeMap's Liberty and Dark), fetching their
# tiles, glyphs and low-zoom underlay from tiles.openfreemap.org: no key, no account, and
# bilingual labels - Safaga renders as "Safaga" over "سفاجا" rather than Arabic-only.
#
# Whatever is configured, the browser is what fetches all of it, so whoever serves it
# learns your users' IP addresses and the z/x/y of the area on screen - roughly where they
# dive, and nothing else. The shipped default contacts one host, and removing the third
# party takes one variable: every one of those maps draws through MapLibre, so a single
# setting reaches all of them - MAP_STYLE_URL for a vector style you serve, or MAP_TILE_URL
# for a raster tile server you run. The two are alternatives rather than layers, so there is
# no second fetch to close off separately. The web app's CSP follows every one of these
# automatically.

# A complete MapLibre style of your own, which wins outright over the raster group below,
# on every map the app draws - set both and those variables are inert rather than layered
# underneath. The dark URL falls back to the light one; setting it alone does nothing and
# says so in the log.
#
# Serve the style's tiles, glyphs and sprite from the style URL's own host. That origin is
# the only one added to the CSP, so a style pulling any of them from a second host draws
# as a blank map with a console error and nothing else to go on. For the same reason, give
# a plain host with no `{s}` subdomain placeholder.
# MAP_STYLE_URL=https://tiles.example.com/styles/mine
# MAP_STYLE_URL_DARK=

# The credit rendered over whichever basemap is active - one variable, not one per mode,
# which is why it is no longer spelled MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION. `[label](url)` becomes a
# link; anything else is plain text.
#
# **Set MAP_STYLE_URL without this and the web app refuses to serve.** It cannot guess
# what your style's licence requires, and crediting the bundled tiles for somebody else's
# would be both false and a licence breach. Note what "refuses to serve" means for a
# container you are watching: configuration is read on the first request, so the process
# starts, every page returns 500, and /healthz keeps answering 200 - Docker will call it
# healthy. Load a page after changing any of this.
#
# Left unset with the raster group below, it stays OpenStreetMap's credit - right if you
# point that group at OpenStreetMap's own tiles, and wrong for anything else. The value
# shown is CARTO's, to go with the block below - yours is whatever you point the map at.
# MAP_ATTRIBUTION="[© OpenStreetMap contributors](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright) [© CARTO](https://carto.com/attributions)"

# The raster escape hatch: `{z}/{x}/{y}` templates, for a tile server you run or a keyed
# provider you prefer. A whole-map mode rather than a second renderer - MapLibre wraps the
# template into a minimal style and draws it the way it draws a vector one. Set one of these
# with no MAP_STYLE_URL above and every map goes raster; set both and the style wins
# outright, leaving these inert. Until August 2026 this group was the map, defaulting to
# OpenStreetMap's standard tiles - keyless, and the only raster basemap still answering an
# unregistered request with place names on it, in Arabic only over Egypt, which is the gap
# the vector default above closes.
#
# `{key}` is replaced by MAP_TILE_API_KEY, so a credential is written once rather than
# pasted into both URLs - the query string is yours because every provider spells the
# parameter differently (CARTO `?key=`, Stadia `?api_key=`). `{r}` marks a provider's
# high-density variant, empty or `@2x` by the display's own pixel ratio; spell it only
# where the variant exists and you are entitled to it. The key reaches the browser, which
# is what fetches the tiles: treat it as a public, domain-restricted credential and
# restrict it at the provider.
#
# CARTO's Positron and Dark Matter below - a matched light/dark pair. A key is free (5M
# tiles a month, emailed back from carto.com/basemaps/apikey) and turns off the watermark
# a keyless request now carries. Set MAP_ATTRIBUTION above to match whatever you point
# these at.
# MAP_TILE_URL=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?key={key}
# MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?key={key}
# MAP_TILE_API_KEY=

# Keep search engines out: robots.txt disallows everything and every page is served with
# `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow`. Worth setting on a private instance that is
# nonetheless reachable from the internet.
# WEB_NOINDEX=true

# Where uploaded dive-computer exports, c-card images, profile pictures and species
# photographs are written, inside the container. The compose file mounts the
# `files-data` volume there, so there is normally nothing to set - change it only if you
# replaced that volume with a bind mount, and then make sure the directory on the host
# is owned by uid 1000 (`sudo chown -R 1000:1000 /srv/opendiving/files`) or the API
# refuses to start. This directory is the second half of your backup; see
# docs/backup-restore.md.
# FILE_STORAGE_DIR=/data/files

# Where those uploads are kept at all: `local` writes them to FILE_STORAGE_DIR above,
# `s3` puts them in an S3-compatible bucket. `local` is the default and is what this
# bundle is built for - one machine, one disk, no credentials to hold.
#
# `s3` is for the install where that does not hold: a platform whose disk attaches to one
# service at a time, while both the api and the worker container need the same files. Any
# S3-compatible store - Cloudflare R2, MinIO, Garage, Ceph, Backblaze B2, AWS. Anything
# other than those two values refuses to start. docs/configuration.md has the whole of it.
# FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3

# The `s3` backend's settings. The first four are required the moment
# FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 - startup refuses and names whichever is missing, rather than
# failing on the first diver's upload. It then writes a probe object and deletes it, so a
# wrong bucket or a credential that cannot write fails here instead of hours later.
#
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL is the full origin including the scheme, e.g.
# https://<account-id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com for Cloudflare R2, or
# http://minio:9000 for a MinIO beside this stack. The bucket has to exist already, and
# should be private: every byte is served through the API, which never hands a browser a
# URL into the store.
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# `auto` is what R2 wants and what every other store ignores. A real AWS bucket needs its
# own region here instead.
# S3_REGION=auto

# Optional key prefix, for sharing one bucket between instances. Prepended to every object
# name; the keys the database holds are unchanged either way, so this can be added or
# removed only by moving the objects.
# S3_PREFIX=

# Switching between the two backends is a copy, not a migration - the key a row carries is
# the same string on both. With both configured and the old one still selected:
#     docker compose exec api python -m src.scripts.migrate_blobs --to s3
# then flip FILE_STORAGE_BACKEND and `docker compose up -d`. It is resumable, and never
# deletes the source.

# Run the migrations yourself instead of on startup:
#   docker compose run --rm api alembic upgrade head
# Leave it alone unless you have a reason - with it off, a container started against a
# schema a version behind fails on the first query that needs the new column.
# MIGRATE_ON_START=false

# The CRUDAdmin interface, OFF by default and a deliberate opt-in: it talks to the models
# directly, bypassing every ownership check the API applies. It is not where you work the
# invite queue - that is the app's own Admin section, which needs none of this. Enabling
# the panel needs the first two lines below, and in production a real password or startup
# refuses. Restrict who can reach it while you are at it - comma-separated, both optional.
# Those two match the *caller's* address only because TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS above is right;
# with a proxy the app hasn't been told about, they match the proxy and lock everybody out.
#
# **Move it off /admin, which is the third line.** The panel still mounts there by default
# and /admin is now a page of the web app, which is where the bundled Caddyfile sends it -
# so enabling the panel without moving it gets you the app, not the panel. Whatever path
# you choose then needs a route of its own to `api:8000`: the Caddyfile carries a
# commented-out block for it, and a proxy of your own needs one location more (see
# docs/reverse-proxy.md). Nothing else has to be split out - /admin reaches the API the way
# every other page of the app does.
# CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED=true
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=a-real-password-not-this-one
# CRUD_ADMIN_MOUNT_PATH=/crud-admin
# ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS=203.0.113.7
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_NETWORKS=10.0.0.0/8

# ============================================================================
# LAN / no domain
# ============================================================================
#
# An instance on a home network with no domain and no certificate. Set DOMAIN to the
# address you reach it at anyway (it is used for nothing else once the three below are
# set), then uncomment all of these:
#
# Serve plain HTTP on port 80 - Caddy cannot get a certificate for an IP address.
# CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS=:80
# The address emailed sign-in links point at, and the web app's own origin. Both default
# to https://${DOMAIN}, which is wrong here in both halves.
# FRONTEND_URL=http://192.168.1.10
# SITE_URL=http://192.168.1.10
# The refresh cookie is `Secure` by default, so a browser drops it on plain HTTP and every
# reload signs the user out.
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=false
#
# Passkeys are not offered on this shape and there is nothing to fix: browsers hand out
# WebAuthn only in a secure context, and an IP address is not a valid passkey domain even
# behind a certificate. The emailed link and the six-digit code beside it are the whole
# sign-in story here. A real hostname with a certificate the browser trusts - a Tailscale
# HTTPS name, an internal CA - makes the instance eligible; point FRONTEND_URL and SITE_URL
# at that https:// name.
# Stops the web app sending `Strict-Transport-Security`. It only ever sends it on a
# request that already arrived over HTTPS, so this matters only if something in front of
# this instance terminates TLS while the LAN address stays HTTP.
# WEB_HSTS=off
